<StaticPage
	title="Disclosure"
	description="How Baggage Gear earns money: affiliate links, sponsored content and ads, and the editorial rules that keep reviews honest."
	icon="coins"
	lead="This site earns money in a few ways. Here they all are, and here is why none of them decide what I recommend."
	c1="#9a4a07"
	c2="#d9822b"
	updated="October 8, 2026"
>
	<ul class="glance">
		<li style="--c1:#9a4a07;--c2:#f0a02a">
			<span class="chip sm"><Icon name="link" size={18} /></span>
			<strong>Affiliate links</strong>
			If you buy through some links, I may earn a commission at no extra cost to you.
		</li>
		<li style="--c1:#4a1d96;--c2:#8b5cf6">
			<span class="chip sm"><Icon name="megaphone" size={18} /></span>
			<strong>Sponsored posts</strong>
			Always labeled “Sponsored” or “Paid Partnership,” and held to the same standards.
		</li>
		<li style="--c1:#0b5f59;--c2:#1fb5a6">
			<span class="chip sm"><Icon name="scale" size={18} /></span>
			<strong>Independent verdicts</strong>
			Money never decides which bag wins, or what I say about its flaws.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<p>
		I’m Iver Calloway, and I want you to know how Baggage Gear pays for itself before you act on anything I
		write. Plain disclosure is part of the job when you’re telling people what to buy.
	</p>

	<h2>Affiliate links</h2>
	<p>
		Some products and services here link to affiliate programs. If you purchase through one of those links, I may
		earn a small commission, <strong>at no additional cost to you</strong>. The programs I take part in include:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>The Amazon Associates Program</li>
		<li>Other travel and retail affiliate programs</li>
	</ul>
	<div class="callout">
		<span class="chip sm"><Icon name="coins" size={18} /></span>
		<div>
			<strong>As an Amazon Associate, I earn from qualifying purchases.</strong> Baggage Gear participates in
			the Amazon Services LLC Associates Program, an affiliate advertising program designed to provide a means
			for sites to earn fees by linking to Amazon.com and affiliated sites.
		</div>
	</div>
	<p>
		A commission never changes a ranking. A bag I recommend earns its place by surviving real travel, and I do
		mention the ones that don’t.
	</p>

	<h2>How I choose and test products</h2>
	<p>My goal is to review bags the way they actually get used. In practice that works like this:</p>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>Durability first.</strong> Wheels, zippers, handles and fabric, judged after real transit abuse.</li>
		<li><strong>Everyday use.</strong> Packing space, access, carrying comfort and fit within airline limits.</li>
		<li><strong>Value.</strong> What the bag costs against what it delivers over many trips.</li>
		<li><strong>Relevance.</strong> Products that suit frequent flyers and vacation travelers.</li>
	</ol>
	<div class="callout warm">
		<span class="chip sm"><Icon name="info" size={18} /></span>
		<div>
			<strong>Tested versus researched.</strong> I put as many bags through real travel as I can, but I can’t
			test everything personally. Where a review rests on research, owner feedback or expert sources instead of
			my own trips, the article says so.
		</div>
	</div>

	<h2>Sponsored content</h2>
	<p>
		Now and then I may publish sponsored posts or work with a brand. These are clearly labeled, and I disclose any
		material connection. A sponsor can supply a product or fund a post, but it cannot approve my verdict or ask me
		to hide a flaw.
	</p>

	<h2>Advertising</h2>
	<p>
		Baggage Gear may display ads through networks such as Google AdSense. Ads can be personalized based on your
		browsing, and I don’t control every ad that appears. Advertising has no influence on my editorial content. Our
		<a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a> explains how cookies work.
	</p>

	<h2>Third-party links</h2>
	<p>
		Links to stores, airlines and other sites are there for your convenience. I don’t control those sites, and a
		link isn’t an endorsement of everything on them. Check their terms and policies before you buy or share
		personal information. Airline baggage rules in particular can change, so confirm fees and limits with the
		airline before you fly.
	</p>

	<h2>Editorial integrity</h2>
	<ul class="ticks">
		<li>Recommendations are based on performance, usability, value and relevance to you.</li>
		<li>Positive and negative points are both reported.</li>
		<li>Affiliate relationships and sponsorships are identified.</li>
		<li>I follow the FTC’s guidelines on endorsements and disclosures.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Questions or updates</h2>
	<p>
		I may update this page as the site changes, and I’ll change the date above when I do. Questions about how
		Baggage Gear earns money? <a href="/contact/">Ask me on the Contact page</a>.
	</p>
</StaticPage>
